The decision-critical concerns:
- assumption realism and positioning: several reviewers question whether a “recoverable/unbiased true negative distribution” is meaningful under real-world exposure/popularity/system intervention biases (especially given evaluation on curated public benchmarks);
- marginal gains: reported gains are often marginal and within variance against strong baselines, with one dataset (Pinterest) standing out without sufficient analysis; 
- unclear practicality/efficiency evidence: the method introduces additional estimation (e.g., exposure propensity network) but lacks convincing quantitative overhead reporting (GFLOPs/memory/epoch time at scale) and discussion of online/streaming deployment.
- presentation issues: notation heaviness and figure/numbering problems further reduce confidence.
